WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.258 --> 00:00:06.300
AMEET JANI: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.420
Today, Jennifer and I are
very excited to walk you

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:10.160
through the BeyondCorp
story from Google.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.298
We want to walk you through how
Google started this journey,

00:00:13.298 --> 00:00:14.840
how we now enable
customers to do it,

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:18.150
and how you can get started in
a crawl, walk, run function.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.573
So let's get started.

00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:21.740
My name's Ameet Jani, and
I'm a product manager here

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.448
on the Google Cloud
team, on the security

00:00:23.448 --> 00:00:26.940
team focused on our zero
trust tools called BeyondCorp.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:28.440
JENNIFER JEREMIAH:
And hi, everyone.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.470
I'm an engagement manager at
Google's professional services

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:31.970
arm.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.160
That's our delivery services.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.800
I look after our key US
insurance and payments

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.310
accounts.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.970
So, Ameet, the philosophy
and notion of zero trust

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.980
is one that we're increasingly
hearing about in the industry.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.970
How do we explain to our
customers what zero trust is

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:49.637
and why it's so important?

00:00:49.637 --> 00:00:51.470
AMEET JANI: Yeah, let's
take a look at that.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.220
There are a lot of
great vendors out there

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.460
that are saying that
they're doing zero trust.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.420
And I think that if you look
at what Google considers really

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.550
the core components that
are required, there four

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:02.180
or five of them that are here.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.510
The first one is that every
zero trust solution really

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.390
needs a strong identity story.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.140
You need to be able
to attest to the user.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.330
You need to be able to
attest the user's metadata.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:10.570
Right?

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:11.695
What groups they belong to?

00:01:11.695 --> 00:01:12.820
What roles do they have?

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.278
And you need to to
be able to start

00:01:14.278 --> 00:01:16.600
to build up an understanding
of the user's behavior.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.730
Does the user normally do this
at 3 o'clock in the morning?

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.710
Does the user normally
go to this site 50 times

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:22.290
and download a lot of files?

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.940
It's really key to understand
all those attributes as part

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:26.505
of the zero-trust story.

00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:27.880
The second part
of this is really

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.910
understanding the context that's
around the user's request.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.530
In this case, we're
saying devices

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:33.775
are a key component of that.

00:01:33.775 --> 00:01:34.900
Is this a corporate device?

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:35.900
Is it a personal device?

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.192
If it's a personal device,
it running an up to date OS?

00:01:38.192 --> 00:01:39.983
Does it have third
party solutions on there

00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:41.860
that we consider really
business critical?

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.590
All of that stuff needs to fit
into the model of understanding

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.852
the context of the request,
along with location,

00:01:46.852 --> 00:01:48.560
and time of day, and
all of other things,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.050
and session lengths.

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:52.450
The third, and I think
key, component of all this

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.070
is what we call a rules engine.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.570
If you're anything
like Google, you

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.210
have thousands of applications
inside of your enterprise,

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.440
and you need a way
to deploy rules

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:02.530
against 1,000 applications
without creating

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.550
1,000 different rules.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.680
And so this simplified
rules engine

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:07.780
is very key to that story.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.405
And then I think
lastly is this idea

00:02:09.405 --> 00:02:10.780
that you should
be able to deploy

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.700
all of your critical
data, whether it's

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.360
applications or VMs, or
APIs, all behind a proxy.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.700
And the proxy in
line to the request

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.280
is able to look at the context
every single time a click is

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.042
made to get to access data.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.000
Is this the right user?

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.310
Are they still coming
from the right group?

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.685
Are they still coming from a
device that's in good state?

00:02:27.685 --> 00:02:29.323
Do they still match
the company policy?

00:02:29.323 --> 00:02:30.615
If not, let's kill the request?

00:02:30.615 --> 00:02:32.860
And if so, let's
allow that request

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.560
to go through, no matter
where that application lives,

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.150
whether it's on GCP, on
another cloud, or on-prem.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.850
It doesn't matter.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.350
JENNIFER JEREMIAH:
So now that we've

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.930
covered the general
components of zero trust,

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.450
can you walk through how
Google implements a zero trust

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.070
model on Google Cloud?

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:49.840
Google calls this BeyondCorp.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.230
Where did the name come from?

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.050
And as a customer, what
is the typical user

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:55.302
journeys that you can see?

00:02:55.302 --> 00:02:56.010
AMEET JANI: Yeah.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.380
Actually, the journey started
for us about 10 years ago

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.280
when we were--

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.575
it was an attempted attack
on Google internal systems.

00:03:03.575 --> 00:03:04.950
The lessons learned
that came out

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.750
of that were that
the network is really

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.130
an artificial bound of security.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.755
Once you're in the network, you
have flat access to everything.

00:03:10.755 --> 00:03:13.002
That was a terrifying
learning for us.

00:03:13.002 --> 00:03:14.460
The other thing
that was really key

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.553
is that we don't care not
only what network you're on,

00:03:17.553 --> 00:03:19.470
we actually don't care
about other attributes.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:20.850
What we really want to
make sure is are you

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.190
the right user, coming from
the right type of device,

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.080
under the right
circumstances in order

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.827
to get access to
that information?

00:03:26.827 --> 00:03:28.410
And from that, the
idea was you should

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.390
be able to work beyond
the corporate walls.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.310
And that got shortened
into BeyondCorp.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.037
We've taken all of
the same tools and all

00:03:34.037 --> 00:03:36.120
the same learnings, and
now we want to expose them

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.985
for customers to use as well.

00:03:37.985 --> 00:03:39.360
So in that vein,
we've introduced

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.310
four or five key
components that make

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.440
the BeyondCorp journey possible
for you and your enterprise.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.440
The first one is what
we call Cloud Identity.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.190
This doesn't necessarily
mean that we have

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:49.440
to be the identity provider.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.600
Use Okta, use
Ping, use Azure AD.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:52.890
It doesn't matter to us.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.470
All that matters to us is that
you federate all that user's

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.420
information to us
so we can understand

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.072
the metadata around that user,
what groups they belong to.

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:02.280
And we can start to build
up an understanding of what

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.260
is normal behavior for all
these different types of users.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:08.225
And we can use that to make
dynamic access decisions.

00:04:08.225 --> 00:04:09.600
The second part
of this is really

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.808
having a ubiquitous endpoint.

00:04:10.808 --> 00:04:13.433
We wanted to make sure that you
should be able to be productive

00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:16.019
from any type of device, whether
it's your corporate device,

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:18.560
or BYOD, or anything
else, as long as we have

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.060
an understanding of what the
security posture of that device

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:21.560
is.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.120
And so for us, we use
Chrome to do that.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.870
The third one here,
and I said it earlier,

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:28.390
was this idea that you should
be able to create access rules

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:30.280
and apply them at
scale across thousands

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.800
and thousands of different
endpoints and applications.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.080
For us, we have a system
called Access Context

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.700
Manager that does exactly that.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.200
Our best practices
and our learnings

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.150
have shown, create
tiers of access.

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:42.220
Keep them very
simple, less than 10.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.020
For Google, we've
gotten down to five.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.390
And we said, make them so
crystal clear that you can just

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.120
apply them to tiers
of applications

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.245
wherever they happen to be.

00:04:50.245 --> 00:04:52.340
So classified applications
get a high level

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:54.250
of rules applied to it.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.485
Lunch menus, and bus schedules,
and everything else get lesser,

00:04:57.485 --> 00:04:59.110
non-confidential
kinds of access levels

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:00.610
applied to it so
you can access from

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:01.990
your personal mobile device.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.760
And again, all of
this stuff needs

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:06.760
to be able to run through
what we call our proxy.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.010
And in our case, it's called
an Identity-Aware Proxy--

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.050
this idea that you
should be able to,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:14.080
in line, again, look at all the
contexts of all those requests

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.110
and still calculate on the fly,
is this still a good request

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.502
or not, and do it with
near zero latency.

00:05:19.502 --> 00:05:21.460
And so for us, that's
our Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.033
Those are the moving pieces
around the BeyondCorp story.

00:05:24.033 --> 00:05:25.950
JENNIFER JEREMIAH: So
if a customer is wanting

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.920
to start implementing
Google's BeyondCorp solution,

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.290
it sounds like setting up
an Identity-Aware Proxy,

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.420
IAP, is critical.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.400
Could you delve
into IAP as well,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.070
and also talk about what use
cases it suits and what it

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:39.782
doesn't?

00:05:39.782 --> 00:05:40.490
AMEET JANI: Yeah.

00:05:40.490 --> 00:05:42.210
And I think you've nailed it.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:44.240
IAP, or the identity
proxy, really

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.660
is core to a lot
of this journey.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:46.160
Right?

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.110
And so let's talk
through what that is.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:50.030
The first thing is
that it sits in front

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.400
of all of the different types
of data and applications

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:54.530
that are out there,
or VMs or APIs.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:56.120
And the basic idea
is you should have

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:59.190
to make no changes to your code,
or to your APIs, or anything

00:05:59.190 --> 00:05:59.690
else.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:01.850
The proxy just
front-loads all of that.

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.100
The second part of it that
I think is really important

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.620
is that this is a
globally-distributed managed

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:07.250
service.

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:09.510
And in fact, this is built
on the same infrastructure

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.232
Google uses to deploy
YouTube or Gmail.

00:06:12.232 --> 00:06:14.690
And so you can understand the
kinds of latency requirements

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.370
that are built around the proxy.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:18.990
And it also comes built
in, then, with security.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.590
So it takes care of all the
authentication, all the context

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:22.350
checking for you.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:23.480
So you don't need to
necessarily do that

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:24.840
through your application.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.050
It also takes care of
more network-centric kinds

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:28.100
of controls as well.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.930
It terminates TLS
for you, and it

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.335
provides a globally-scaled
DDOS protection.

00:06:32.335 --> 00:06:33.710
Again, this is
the same front-end

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.710
we use to protect our
core businesses at Google

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:38.220
from state-sponsored
DDOS attacks,

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.095
and we extend that to
customers who are using

00:06:40.095 --> 00:06:42.260
those applications behind IAP.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.870
The other part of this
is that the proxy needs

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:47.072
to be able to protect
any type of application

00:06:47.072 --> 00:06:48.530
or any type of
data, whether that's

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.980
web applications, whether
that's SSH, or TCP, or RDP.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:53.160
It doesn't matter.

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.100
It should all flow through the
proxy where we can go ahead

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.142
and do all those context
checks before we forward

00:06:57.142 --> 00:06:58.040
the traffic on.

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.740
And again, customers come
to us all the time and say,

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:02.240
I'm just riding
my cloud journey.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.948
My applications have everywhere.

00:07:03.948 --> 00:07:05.990
The proxy needs to be able
to distribute and take

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:07.730
care of that for
everybody, no matter

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.060
where the application is.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.560
JENNIFER JEREMIAH:
A common question

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:13.540
that I hear from
customers is that users

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:16.450
are accessing applications
from various devices--

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.730
mobiles, tablets, desktops.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:21.730
How do we monitor the health of
all those devices at the user

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.270
level?

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.020
AMEET JANI: Yeah, this
is a common problem

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.210
we found when Google was going
through the journey ourselves,

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:29.502
and now we're hearing customers
tell us the same thing.

00:07:29.502 --> 00:07:31.680
Users have multiple
devices, and how do I

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:34.290
make sure that it's the right
user using the right device.

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.660
And what are the
attributes of that device?

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.310
It's not uncommon
today for all of us

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:41.430
to have a corporate device,
a personal device at home

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.020
that we want to
check email from,

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:46.710
even our own mobile devices or
devices given to us by work.

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:50.560
And we need to go to attest
to the status of that device

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.040
before we allow access to
systems based on that device

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.070
itself.

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:57.235
And so to solve that problem,
Google for itself, and now

00:07:57.235 --> 00:07:58.860
for customers, has
introduced something

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.870
called endpoint verification.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.910
Endpoint verification
simply sits inside

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:04.890
of a Chrome browser on any OS--

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:07.980
Mac, Windows, Linux, Chrome OS--
or on a mobile device outside

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:09.420
of the Chrome browser--

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:10.920
both iOS and Android.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.170
And it collects information
about that device.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:16.290
And it sends that information
on every single request

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:17.410
made by the end user.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.560
So if I started an
app and click around,

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.870
both my user information,
my user session data,

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:24.360
and the device data is
sent on those requests.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.510
And it helps you understand,
is this a corporate device?

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:29.370
If it is, then I
know that it's safe.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.570
I can allow this device
to do a lot more than,

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:32.880
for example, a BYOD device.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.530
I still want users
to be productive.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.340
They can still access
Gmail, for example,

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:37.590
or any of their email clients.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:38.730
But we don't really
want them to get

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.147
into too much of
confidential data

00:08:40.147 --> 00:08:41.549
with financial information.

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:43.260
So you can make that
kind of distinction

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.008
understanding the
device posture.

00:08:45.008 --> 00:08:47.550
And then, of course, you can
use that device posture not just

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.740
in accessing applications
but in accessing Saas,

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:52.905
for example, with Gmail,
or for accessing APIs.

00:08:52.905 --> 00:08:55.200
You can say, only
employees can be

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.295
able to access public APIs that
are out there if they're coming

00:08:58.295 --> 00:08:59.420
from a device that I trust.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:03.105
And so this information gets
reused in many different ways.

00:09:03.105 --> 00:09:04.480
JENNIFER JEREMIAH:
So, Ameet this

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:05.990
sounds great for
customers that are

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:07.898
using Google Cloud's Platform.

00:09:07.898 --> 00:09:09.440
What about customers
that are looking

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:12.830
to integrate IAP with their
non-Google Cloud platform?

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:15.860
Is this even possible, and
are there still the security

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:18.002
benefits of BeyondCorp?

00:09:18.002 --> 00:09:18.710
AMEET JANI: Yeah.

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:20.810
In fact, I would argue
that many of our customers

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.310
today are doing exactly this.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:24.540
They're saying, yes, I'm
starting my cloud journey

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:25.760
at either GCP--

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:27.830
Google Cloud is
one of my clouds,

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:29.720
or even if it's the
cloud I've chosen,

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.850
I still have most of
my workloads on-prem.

00:09:31.850 --> 00:09:36.140
And I want a uniform and a
ubiquitous solution for all

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:37.315
of my zero trust models.

00:09:37.315 --> 00:09:38.690
And so you can
see all the things

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:40.535
we've talked about
still apply here.

00:09:40.535 --> 00:09:43.160
The first thing is, as long as
I understand the endpoint status

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:44.660
based on endpoint
verification and I

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.060
know about the user,
all of that information

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.100
is sent to the Google
front-end, again

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.163
where we can do all the
analysis on the traffic.

00:09:51.163 --> 00:09:52.580
Is the user doing
normal behavior?

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:54.038
Is it coming from
the right device?

00:09:54.038 --> 00:09:56.170
Has anything changed
since the last click?

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:57.680
And assuming
everything goes right,

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.890
and the proxy determines
all the rules are matched

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.390
and nothing looks fishy, it
just simply forwards the traffic

00:10:02.390 --> 00:10:04.570
back to wherever the
application, again

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:06.380
whether that's on-prem
or in another cloud.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.660
We have many customers
running in other clouds,

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:11.948
and they use this solution
to solve that problem.

00:10:11.948 --> 00:10:14.490
JENNIFER JEREMIAH: I think it'll
be helpful for our customers

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:15.880
to see this in action.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.238
Let's go into the Google
Cloud Platform Console

00:10:18.238 --> 00:10:20.280
and start to walk through
the tactical steps that

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:21.742
are needed to set this up.

00:10:21.742 --> 00:10:23.200
AMEET JANI: Yeah,
I like that idea.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:25.050
In fact, this is a
great opportunity

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:28.420
to not only do that, but to
walk through the baby steps

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:31.260
that an enterprise would need
to start to transition through

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:32.940
to get to a zero trust model.

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:35.400
The frequent thing
I hear is, Ameet,

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:37.122
I buy the zero trust
BeyondCorp story.

00:10:37.122 --> 00:10:39.330
I really understand what
you're saying, and I'm sold.

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:40.580
But how do I even get started?

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:41.700
I have 5,000 applications.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:42.700
Half of them are legacy.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:44.520
I have three different
identity providers

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:46.620
from all these mergers
and acquisitions.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.450
And I think that's
really the crux

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.617
and the first step of
understanding what's going on.

00:10:50.617 --> 00:10:52.410
You need to understand,
who are your users,

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.630
and what are the devices
that are accessing data,

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:57.393
whether they're yours or
managed by somebody else?

00:10:57.393 --> 00:10:59.310
And so I would argue
that any customer getting

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.310
started on this journey
should spend a few weeks

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:02.470
understanding exactly that.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:04.262
So let's walk through
what that looks like.

00:11:04.262 --> 00:11:06.930
And so in this example, I think
it's really important that you

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:09.747
understand RBAC comes
first and understanding

00:11:09.747 --> 00:11:12.330
who are in the groups that are
assigned there, whether they're

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.663
on Google, or whether they're
coming from Okta or Azure,

00:11:14.663 --> 00:11:15.420
it doesn't matter.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.003
The group membership
needs to present,

00:11:17.003 --> 00:11:19.390
and you need to be able to
see are all the right members

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:21.223
part of the right groups,
and are they here?

00:11:21.223 --> 00:11:24.060
For example, I can see these are
the members of my engineering

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:24.850
team.

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:25.980
This is really
critical because you're

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.938
going to later on want
to create granular level

00:11:27.938 --> 00:11:29.360
access for these sets of users.

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:31.210
And you're going to want
to use groups to do that.

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:31.980
The other one is
you're going to want

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:34.290
to get a sense of all the other
users in your organization.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.110
Is everybody here that's
supposed to get access?

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:37.410
Are all my employees present?

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.807
In my world, all my
41 employees are here.

00:11:40.807 --> 00:11:43.140
But you want a deeper level
understanding of those users

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:44.050
as well.

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:45.690
So here I can see
in my enterprise

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:46.970
I've got 41 employees.

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:49.262
Two recently left the
company, so they're suspended.

00:11:49.262 --> 00:11:51.345
So I get a sense of all
the people that are there.

00:11:51.345 --> 00:11:52.950
Are they using second factor?

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:54.370
This is really key.

00:11:54.370 --> 00:11:55.602
So you can see here Alice is.

00:11:55.602 --> 00:11:57.060
Many of the other
employees aren't.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.230
So I can go back and start
to nag those employees

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:01.578
and say, you really need
to enroll in second factor.

00:12:01.578 --> 00:12:03.120
And you can also
get a sense of, what

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.355
are the types of devices
that are accessing the data?

00:12:05.355 --> 00:12:07.230
Again, maybe they're in
your corporate fleet,

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:07.830
maybe they're not.

00:12:07.830 --> 00:12:08.500
It doesn't matter.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:10.490
But you need to get a
sense of how that looks.

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:12.417
In fact, let's double
click on what that is.

00:12:12.417 --> 00:12:14.250
The second part of this
is you should really

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.380
understand the device
status and the device state

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:17.380
for all these employees.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:19.590
Here you can see my devices
running an up to date

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:20.830
version of macOS.

00:12:20.830 --> 00:12:22.170
It's a company device.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.540
You can see I have other BYOD
devices that are in here.

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:24.975
That's a concern.

00:12:24.975 --> 00:12:26.517
And I have some that
are out of date.

00:12:26.517 --> 00:12:27.960
This is terrifying to me.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:30.190
Nobody should be
accessing with 10.14.6.

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:31.740
It's got a known bug in it.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:33.660
And so you can start
to passively watch

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:35.970
all these trends over
a course of X weeks

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.930
and understand, what
does the fleet look like,

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:39.720
and what is the health
of the users that

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:42.090
are accessing my
infrastructure, so I

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:44.762
can use that to
make rules later on.

00:12:44.762 --> 00:12:46.220
JENNIFER JEREMIAH:
So these rules--

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:50.512
how granular are these security
rules that we're setting up?

00:12:50.512 --> 00:12:51.220
AMEET JANI: Yeah.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:52.800
Everything that you've seen--

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.300
all the attributes that you've
seen, plus device attributes,

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:57.160
and location attributes,
and time of day attributes

00:12:57.160 --> 00:12:59.480
could all be used to make
the kinds of access rules.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:01.790
And then you can apply
them against applications.

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:02.950
So let's walk through.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:05.573
Now that I've gotten a sense
of what my data set looks

00:13:05.573 --> 00:13:07.990
like-- who are the users, what
are the groups that they're

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:10.190
accessing from, what
are the devices?

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:12.790
Let's get a sense of how we can
use exactly that information.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:15.970
So you saw that I got a sense
of make my corporate fleet.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:18.890
And I understand the kinds of
OS that they're running and are

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.440
they corporate devices or not.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.320
Let's codify this in rules.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:24.460
So here you see I've
already created three rules.

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:25.980
And they're called
secure device--

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:27.730
which may be a corporate device.

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:31.090
I have a contractors tier, which
is a device I don't control.

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:32.080
And I may have BYOD--

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:35.090
again, a device I don't control
but I want to allow access.

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:37.880
And you can also geo rules,
and IP rules, and et cetera.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:40.155
So here you can see
for company rules

00:13:40.155 --> 00:13:42.030
I can get a sense of
what are those policies.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:43.480
I've really insisted
on screen lock.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:44.440
I want to make sure
that I've marked it

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:45.495
as a corporate device.

00:13:45.495 --> 00:13:46.870
And I want to make
sure that it's

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:49.130
wearing the latest and
greatest OS versions

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.460
for both Windows,
Mac, and Chrome,

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:54.160
because by company policy
all these devices should

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:55.230
be updated.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.370
For contractors, I don't
necessarily control that.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:59.453
They may be a couple of
weeks behind in upgrading,

00:13:59.453 --> 00:14:01.760
and I can get a sense of
what that looks like here.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:02.730
This screen lock is enabled.

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:03.900
It has to be approved by me.

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:05.595
And for BYOD, I
just want to make

00:14:05.595 --> 00:14:06.370
sure screen lock is enabled.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:08.870
Because somebody is going to
use that device to check email.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.780
And I want to make sure that if
they turn around at Starbucks,

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:15.040
for example, that they don't
necessarily have that machine

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:17.870
swiped and somebody can just
access that information.

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:21.368
So that's, in essence, what
you would do with those rules.

00:14:21.368 --> 00:14:22.410
JENNIFER JEREMIAH: Great.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.060
And what are the
types of applications

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:27.235
that make good starting
points for this journey?

00:14:27.235 --> 00:14:29.110
AMEET JANI: This is
actually the core of what

00:14:29.110 --> 00:14:30.730
every customer always asks me.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.860
They say, I buy the vision.

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:34.990
Yes, I can go and understand
my corporate fleet

00:14:34.990 --> 00:14:36.527
and my health of my devices.

00:14:36.527 --> 00:14:38.360
But I really don't even
know where to start.

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:39.820
And so some of
the best practices

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:41.125
we've found from our
Google experience,

00:14:41.125 --> 00:14:42.910
and what we tell our
customers, and where

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:45.492
we find we have success
is really do two things.

00:14:45.492 --> 00:14:47.575
One is you can start with
any type of application,

00:14:47.575 --> 00:14:49.420
but starting with
web applications

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.370
gives you the biggest
bang for the value.

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:54.270
A, they're meant to be
deployed on the web.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:56.960
B, they have a subset of
users who need to access them.

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.720
So you can go ahead
and find out is there

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:00.460
a subset of HR
contractors, for example,

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:01.460
who need to access this?

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:03.353
Are there contractors
outside of my org

00:15:03.353 --> 00:15:05.020
that I don't want to
give VPN access to,

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:07.040
but they can access
only one application?

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.760
These are great sorts of
proof of concept applications

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.247
that you can use to really
test out the zero trust value.

00:15:12.247 --> 00:15:14.830
And again, you can do this all
without necessarily turning off

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:15.870
the VPN.

00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.370
You can use it to test it out
and trust that you believe it,

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:20.495
and then you can go
forward from there.

00:15:20.495 --> 00:15:22.370
So let's get a sense of
what that looks like.

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:24.280
You saw that I created
the rules previously

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:25.510
with Access Context Manager.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.802
Now, let's come in and see
how we can apply those rules

00:15:27.802 --> 00:15:28.940
against applications.

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:30.790
So again, the Identity-Aware
Proxy comes up.

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:32.260
First thing you see
is that you don't

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.240
necessarily to apply them
against just Google Cloud

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:34.740
resources.

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:36.695
They can be deployed
against any resource.

00:15:36.695 --> 00:15:38.570
And here you can see I
have web applications.

00:15:38.570 --> 00:15:41.080
This is in essence a
dashboard all the applications

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:42.813
I have in my environment.

00:15:42.813 --> 00:15:44.480
You see some of them
are running on GCP.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.717
I have some running on AWS here.

00:15:46.717 --> 00:15:48.050
And I have some running on-prem.

00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:49.790
And the idea is it doesn't
matter where they're running,

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:51.620
you want to create
a uniform dashboard.

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:55.160
The same is true, by the way,
of RDP, or SSH, of TCP sessions.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.470
I have all these resources
located all over the place,

00:15:57.470 --> 00:15:59.510
but again, I want to
create uniform access

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:00.680
to those resources.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:02.930
So let's actually walk through
what an example of that

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:03.950
looks like.

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:06.560
The first one is I have a
compensation application.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:08.750
It reveals financial
information about my employees

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:10.360
and other kinds of things.

00:16:10.360 --> 00:16:13.340
I need to make sure that only
members of the HR contracting

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:15.900
group should be
allowed to access this.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.380
So you can see here I
said HR contractors can

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:19.640
come in and access it.

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:22.250
But they can only come in if
they're accessing it from what

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:23.480
we consider a secure device.

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:25.760
If you remember, we previously
set what that means.

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:28.310
We want to make sure that this
information can never leak,

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:30.380
and the devices have
to be safe devices.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:33.230
And so now only six members
of the HR contracting

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:35.967
group coming in from corporate
devices can see that.

00:16:35.967 --> 00:16:38.300
Here's another example where
we have a Jira application.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:40.400
And we want to make sure all
the employees inside the company

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.710
can see project statuses
and things like that.

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:43.990
You can add that rule as well.

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:46.407
You can say anybody who is an
authenticated and legitimate

00:16:46.407 --> 00:16:49.520
company user, meaning they're
coming from the right domain,

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:51.625
should be able to
access this application.

00:16:51.625 --> 00:16:53.000
Again, but they
need to be coming

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:55.710
in from at least a device that's
running a minimum security

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:56.210
trust value.

00:16:56.210 --> 00:16:59.010
Here again, screen
lock is required.

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:01.242
So they can access
this application.

00:17:01.242 --> 00:17:02.825
And then, of course,
we can apply this

00:17:02.825 --> 00:17:04.105
at scale versus other things.

00:17:04.105 --> 00:17:05.480
So let's get a
sense of what that

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:06.920
looks like from an
employee's point of view.

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:07.760
Here's Bob.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:08.930
Bob as an employee.

00:17:08.930 --> 00:17:11.155
Instead of logging into
a VPN and then navigating

00:17:11.155 --> 00:17:13.280
to the right application,
they fire up the browser.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:15.740
They just enter the URL
of the Jira application,

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:16.820
and they're logged in.

00:17:16.820 --> 00:17:19.490
It was just like they would
access Gmail or any type

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:20.780
of SaaS application.

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:23.484
Internal systems are now
easy to access for employees,

00:17:23.484 --> 00:17:25.359
but you're not reducing
the security posture.

00:17:25.359 --> 00:17:27.260
In fact, you're
strengthening it by saying

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:28.940
only these users
from this time of day

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:30.840
should be able to
access this application.

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.340
And so that's a
really key component

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:34.463
of the zero trust story.

00:17:34.463 --> 00:17:35.380
JENNIFER JEREMIAH: OK.

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:36.940
So now that we've
selected and we've

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:40.502
secured our key
applications, is that enough?

00:17:40.502 --> 00:17:41.960
AMEET JANI: Actually,
I would argue

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:43.970
there are steps that are even
more important than anything

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:44.762
we've talked about.

00:17:44.762 --> 00:17:46.650
And we have it here
listed as step four,

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:49.130
but I think you could even
think of this as step zero

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:50.237
in all of this.

00:17:50.237 --> 00:17:52.070
It's a well-known fact
that numerous studies

00:17:52.070 --> 00:17:54.020
have been published
that the biggest attack

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:56.180
vector, especially the
most successful attack

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:59.480
vectors against your enterprise,
are phished credentials.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:01.700
And so I would argue
that session controls

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:04.882
and second factor are really
key to this entire story.

00:18:04.882 --> 00:18:06.840
And so we won't touch on
session controls here,

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:08.000
but it's really
important understand,

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.280
if somebody is accessing
highly confidential data,

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:12.410
maybe those sessions should
be 10 minutes, or 20 minutes,

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:12.910
or one hour.

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:15.110
They shouldn't be
24 hour sessions.

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:17.040
And the same will be
true for second factor.

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:19.470
Here we'll show you an example
of what that looks like.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:21.890
So at Google, we
really, really recommend

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:23.180
that second factor be there.

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:25.600
Second factor is better than
no second factor, of course,

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:27.640
but we really push
this idea of FIDO keys.

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:29.870
So here you can see
for my applications,

00:18:29.870 --> 00:18:32.120
I'm going to say I'm
enforcing second factor,

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:33.620
and it's going to
start in two weeks

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:35.270
so I have time to nag everybody.

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:37.070
Any new employee that
joins has one week

00:18:37.070 --> 00:18:39.930
to be able to register their
MFA or get the key in the mail,

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.150
if they're not in the office.

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:43.940
And for us, we're saying
security key is really

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:45.480
the best story that you have.

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:45.980
Right?

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:48.260
It doesn't matter-- OTP
codes are being phished.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:50.490
There are many public
examples of that.

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:52.730
And so now I've enabled
for all the applications

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:54.590
in my enterprise
this idea that they

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:57.520
require second factor to get
access to the applications.

00:18:57.520 --> 00:18:59.780
I can go one step further
and create granular levels

00:18:59.780 --> 00:19:01.275
of session control as well.

00:19:01.275 --> 00:19:03.650
I can say, for example, that
all confidential data should

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:05.200
have short session lengths.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.370
My cafeteria menus are
not confidential data,

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:09.970
and so it's OK if they
don't require second factor

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:12.000
or if the session
lasts 24 hours.

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.270
I think that's really
step one of this journey.

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:16.910
The second part of
this is actually,

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:19.467
I think, more fundamental.

00:19:19.467 --> 00:19:21.800
We've talked so far about
different types of application

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:23.130
controlled by the data.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:24.620
Think about
applications with debt,

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:26.960
with confidential
information, or VMs that

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:28.490
can shut down environments.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:30.980
But really, the key
that's important

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:33.440
here is that you protect
access to the production

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:35.000
systems themselves.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:36.530
Think about this
idea that somebody

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:38.690
will deal to log
into your environment

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:41.240
and start shutting off VMs or
containers that are running

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:42.932
really important
data, or that they

00:19:42.932 --> 00:19:44.390
can come into your
identity systems

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:45.970
and just start to
create identities

00:19:45.970 --> 00:19:47.780
that you don't
control that now have

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:49.050
backdoor access to everything.

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.508
And so what we really say is
that the zero trust BeyondCorp

00:19:51.508 --> 00:19:54.110
model should really extend
to your cloud consoles,

00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:56.360
your administrative consoles,
because that's really

00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:59.113
bread and butter
security to your company.

00:19:59.113 --> 00:20:00.030
JENNIFER JEREMIAH: OK.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:02.530
So SecOps and
networking are going

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:05.680
to be critical to implementing
the BeyondCorp solution.

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:10.250
To recap our key steps, it's
critical to, number one--

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:11.980
understand to your
users are and what

00:20:11.980 --> 00:20:14.590
is the inventory of devices
that are being used.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:16.540
Number two-- get granular
with your security

00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:19.690
by setting up context-aware
access level controls.

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:22.240
Number three--
identify your POC apps.

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:24.700
Web applications that have
a common group of users

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.750
make a simple place to start.

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:30.430
Number four-- strengthen
user authentication

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:33.430
through practices like
second factor authentication.

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:37.132
And number five-- protect
your internal systems.

00:20:37.132 --> 00:20:37.840
AMEET JANI: Yeah.

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:40.035
I think that's a perfect
encapsulation of everything

00:20:40.035 --> 00:20:41.410
that we've talked
through so far.

00:20:41.410 --> 00:20:43.118
It's a great place to
start this journey.

00:20:43.118 --> 00:20:45.370
I know the journey to zero
trust seems overwhelming,

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:48.220
but this really is a
great list to say, let's

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:49.425
at least crawl, walk, run.

00:20:49.425 --> 00:20:50.800
Let's start with
the crawl phase.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:52.378
These are the great
steps to do it.

00:20:52.378 --> 00:20:53.920
In fact, there are
numerous customers

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:55.712
that have already
started this journey that

00:20:55.712 --> 00:20:57.245
are publicly referencable.

00:20:57.245 --> 00:20:58.870
There are thousands
of Google customers

00:20:58.870 --> 00:21:00.787
that are now starting
this journey.

00:21:00.787 --> 00:21:01.870
These are the public ones.

00:21:01.870 --> 00:21:03.190
Let me give you a
couple of examples

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:05.860
to show you the different types
of use cases customers have.

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:07.660
Last year, Airbnb
was on stage with us,

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:09.160
where they talk
through this example

00:21:09.160 --> 00:21:11.980
of they have contractors
who are not Airbnb employees

00:21:11.980 --> 00:21:15.070
accessing key customer
service applications that

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:16.323
are hosted on AWS.

00:21:16.323 --> 00:21:17.740
They don't want
to give them VPNs.

00:21:17.740 --> 00:21:19.823
They don't want to build
an authentication system.

00:21:19.823 --> 00:21:23.110
This BeyondCorp story provided
an easy way for it to front

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:27.110
those AWS hosted applications
for those sets of users.

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:28.630
Another example is Home Depot.

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:31.510
Home Depot has 400,000
users, including

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:33.760
the ones on the store floors.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:36.790
The idea should be that they're
seeing confidential inventory

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:37.330
data.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:38.950
This is core to what
Home Depot does.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:41.283
We need to make sure the
devices they're accessing from,

00:21:41.283 --> 00:21:43.260
the locations that
they're accessing from

00:21:43.260 --> 00:21:44.920
are relevant and stay safe.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:47.080
And as the employees
that work in the store

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:49.390
are sometimes transient, we
need to make sure that when

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:51.220
they leave working
for this company

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:53.543
that you can shut down
access for those users.

00:21:53.543 --> 00:21:55.210
JENNIFER JEREMIAH:
And of course, Ameet,

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:57.320
it's important to
include Google here.

00:21:57.320 --> 00:21:59.770
Google was the first
consumer of BeyondCorp.

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:03.070
It's our homegrown way
of securing our systems.

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:04.220
Thanks for tuning in.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:06.095
We hope that you've
learned a little bit more

00:22:06.095 --> 00:22:08.682
about Google's
BeyondCorp and IAP.

00:22:08.682 --> 00:22:10.390
You now have the
critical building blocks

00:22:10.390 --> 00:22:12.848
that are needed to start setting
up this important security

00:22:12.848 --> 00:22:13.570
service.

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:16.540
And you've got product managers
and professional services

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:18.470
organization here to help you.

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.020
Thanks.

